The economic value of volunteer work in the US is estimated at $167 billion per year by Independent Sector, using the average wage of comparable paid work.
The Red Cross provides 90% of domestic disaster relief in the US entirely through its volunteer workforce — a service that would cost tens of billions annually if staffed professionally.
Community organisations with active volunteer bases show 40% lower rates of social isolation, and lower local crime rates, according to Robert Putnam's Bowling Alone research on social capital.
